b. provision of access to information on protection implementation;
c. protection from threats or violence by perpetrators and other parties as well as recurrence of violence;
d. protection of identity confidentiality;
e. protection from attitudes and behavior of law enforcement officers that demean victims;
f. protection from loss of employment, job transfer, education, or political access; andg. protection of victims and/or reporters from criminal prosecution or civil lawsuits for sexual violence crimes that have been reported;
4. The right to recovery as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c includes:a. medical rehabilitation;
b. mental and social rehabilitation;
c. social empowerment;
d. restitution and/or compensation;
e. social reintegration; andf. obtaining skills training for economic improvement for vulnerable women.

Article 9

1. The Regional Government carries out integrated efforts to prevent violence, exploitation and discrimination against women in the Region.
2. Prevention of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women is intended to create a safe environment for women and to reduce vulnerability to violence, exploitation and discrimination for vulnerable women groups.
3. Vulnerable women groups as referred to in paragraph (2) include:a. women in disaster and social conflict situations; b. women with disabilities;
c. women with Human Immunodeficiency Virus/Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome (HIV/AIDS); d. women migrant workers;
e. girls;
f. women heads of households;
g. domestic women workers;
h. home-based women workers;
i. elderly women;
j. informal women workers;
k. women in situations of intolerance; andl. other vulnerable women groups.
4. Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are implemented by means including:a. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of prevention of acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination based on partnership patterns;
b. conducting socialization regarding laws and regulations related to the protection of women's rights.  c. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system for prevention of violence, exploitation and discrimination;
d. providing ease of access to obtain civil rights, education, health, employment, training and funding, welfare improvement and services;
e. developing communication media, information, education, and public campaigns through media;
f. developing educational materials and curriculum; g. developing safe transportation systems and public spaces;
h. building early detection systems, security and integrated complaint services in industrial areas, companies, residential environments, educational environments, Islamic boarding schools and other public spaces;
i. providing consultation services for families, prospective brides and grooms, students, Islamic boarding school students, youth, university students and vulnerable groups;
j. encouraging behavioral change of parents and the community;
k. developing policies related to regencies, sub-districts, schools, Islamic boarding schools, and universities that are safe and friendly for women and integrated with regional spatial planning policies;
l. forming and developing cadres, communities, and groups from among youth, students, Islamic boarding school students, university students, journalists, social media, educators, and religious leaders for prevention;
m. conducting education and advocacy to owners, managers and/or users of social media;
n. developing protection systems and special support for vulnerable women groups;
o. conducting awareness-raising for perpetrators;
p. increasing the capacity of Regional Apparatus, government agencies, educational institutions, religious institutions, health service provider institutions, professional institutions, business world, service institutions, Islamic boarding schools, youth organizations, legal aid organizations, media, religious leaders, community leaders and communities; andq. forming cooperation networks in prevention efforts from acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination with law enforcement officers, government agencies, Regional Apparatus, community institutions, educational institutions, community leaders, religious institutions, health service provider institutions, professional institutions, business world, service institutions, Islamic boarding schools, youth organizations, legal aid organizations, communities, and media.
5. Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (4) may involve Regional Apparatus, agencies, community institutions, educational institutions, community leaders, family learning centers, religious institutions, health service provider institutions, professional institutions, business world, service institutions, Islamic boarding schools, youth organizations, legal aid organizations, communities, and media.
6. Further provisions regarding efforts to prevent violence, exploitation, and discrimination as referred to in paragraph (4) are regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Article 12

1. Forms of services to victims of violence, exploitation, and discrimination include: a. receiving reports and outreach to Victims;
b. providing information about Victims' Rights;
c. facilitating the provision of health services;
d. facilitating the provision of psychological strengthening services;
e. facilitating the provision of psychosocial services, social rehabilitation, and social reintegration;
f. providing legal services;
g. identifying economic empowerment needs;
h. identifying temporary shelter needs for Victims and Victims' Families that need to be immediately fulfilled;
i. facilitating the needs of Victims with Disabilities;
j. coordinating and cooperating on the fulfillment of Victims' Rights with other institutions; and k. monitoring the fulfillment of Victims' Rights by law enforcement officials during the judicial process.
2. The implementation of forms of services to victims of violence, exploitation and discrimination as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 3

1. The Implementation of Protection and Empowerment of Women is intended to realize protection for women from acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination as well as to increase the capacity and welfare of Women in various aspects of life.
2. The implementation of protection and empowerment of women aims to: a. prevent all forms of violence, exploitation, and discrimination against women;
b. provide services and recovery for victims; c. fulfill the special needs of women; and d. improve the quality of life of women.
3. The scope of this Regional Regulation includes: a. women's rights;
b. efforts to protect women;
c. empowerment of women;
d. obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government;
e. institutions;
f. coordination and cooperation;
g. participation;
h. monitoring, evaluation, and reporting;
i. awards; and j. funding.

Article 4

1. Every person, both women and men, has the right and responsibility to create an environment that supports gender equality and the Empowerment of Women in various aspects of life including rights for every mother.
2. Every woman has the right: a. to life;
b. to form a family and continue offspring;
c. to develop herself;
d. to obtain justice;
e. to personal freedom;
f. to security;
g. to welfare; and h. to participate in government.
3. The right to life as referred to in paragraph (2) letter a includes: a. the right to maintain life and improve her standard of living;
b. the right to obtain peace of mind, security, peace, happiness, physical and spiritual welfare; and c. the right to a good and healthy environment.
4. The right to form a family and continue offspring as referred to in paragraph (2) letter b is the right to form a family and continue offspring through lawful marriage in accordance with religion and the provisions of laws and regulations.
5. The right to develop herself as referred to in paragraph (2) letter c includes: a. the right of women to her basic needs to grow and develop properly;
b. the right to protection for the development of her personality, to obtain education, educate herself and improve the quality of life to become a faithful, pious, responsible, noble, happy, and prosperous human being in accordance with human rights;
c. the right to develop and obtain benefits from science and technology, arts and culture in accordance with human dignity for the welfare of herself, the nation, and humanity;
d. the right to communicate and obtain information necessary to develop her personality and social environment in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
e. the right to seek, obtain, own, store, process, and convey information using all types of available means in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
f. the right to fight for the right to develop herself, both personally and collectively, to build society, nation, and state in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
g. the right to perform social work in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
h. the right to participate in the planning and implementation of government policies in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
i. the right to organize and form organizations in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations; and j. the right to organize education and teaching in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms.
6. The right to obtain justice as referred to in paragraph (2) letter d includes: a. the right to receive equal treatment without discrimination;
b. the right to obtain justice;
c. the right to submit applications, complaints, and lawsuits, whether in criminal, civil, or administrative cases; and d. the right to be tried through a free and impartial judicial process, in accordance with procedural law that guarantees objective examination by honest and fair judges to obtain a just and correct decision.
7. The right to personal freedom as referred to in paragraph (2) letter e includes: a. the right not to be enslaved or subjugated, trafficked, and all acts with similar purposes;
b. the right to personal integrity, both spiritual and physical;
c. the right not to be the object of research without her consent; and d. the right to freely express and convey opinions with due regard to religious values, morality, order, public interest, and national integrity.
8. The right to security as referred to in paragraph (2) letter f includes: a. the right to protection of her person, family, honor, dignity, and property rights;
b. the right to recognition before the law as a human person; and c. the right to be free from threats to do or not do something that violates religious, social, and legal norms. (9) The right to welfare as referred to in paragraph (2) letter g includes: a. the right to have welfare, either alone or together; and b. the right to perform work and receive fair wages in accordance with her work that is comparable and commensurate with human dignity in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
10. The right to participate in government as referred to in paragraph (2) letter h includes: a. the right to be elected and to vote in general elections, regional head elections, and/or other political office elections based on equal rights through direct, general, free, secret, honest and fair voting according to applicable laws and regulations;
b. the right to pursue a career in government; and c. the right to convey aspirations directly or through intermediaries in government.

Article 16

1. The implementation of Women's Empowerment in the field of health as referred to in Article 15 paragraph (2) letter c number 1 is carried out by Regional Apparatus that administer government affairs in the field of health.
2. Forms of empowerment in the field of health as referred to in paragraph (1) are in the form of health services that are healthy, safe, and free from coercion and/or violence, including for persons with disabilities, the elderly, and women with psychological problems.
3. Health services as referred to in paragraph (2) include: a. health services according to the life cycle starting from health services for women in infancy and childhood, adolescence, adulthood and old age;
b. maternal health services starting from the pre-pregnancy period, pregnancy, childbirth and postpartum period;
c. contraceptive and sexual health services;
d. reproductive health services;
e. health services in family planning; f. nutrition services;
g. dental and oral health services;
h. vision and hearing health services; i. mental health services;
j. communicable disease control services and non-communicable disease control services;
k. family health services;
l. school health services;
m. occupational health services;
n. sports health services;
o. environmental health services;
p. matra health services;
q. health services in disasters;
r. blood services;
s. organ and/or body tissue transplantation services, cell-based and/or stem cell therapy, as well as reconstructive and aesthetic plastic surgery;
t. traditional health services;
u. other health services;
v. services and provision of adequate lactation rooms in every Regional Government agency, public facilities and industries; and w. health services are provided taking into account the special needs of persons with disabilities, the elderly, and women with psychological problems to ensure accessibility and equal quality of services.
4. Health services as referred to in paragraph (2) are implemented through promotive, preventive, curative, and rehabilitative approaches.

Article 30

1. The Regional Government in an effort to provide and implement protection services for women victims of violence, exploitation and discrimination establishes KPPAD/KPPAK.
2. KPPAD/KPPAK as referred to in paragraph (1) is under and responsible to the Regional Apparatus that carries out governmental affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection through the Village Head/Sub-District Head with the knowledge of the local Sub-District Head.
3. KPPAD/KPPAK as referred to in paragraph (2) has the duties of:a. conducting advocacy, mediation, socialization, and facilitation of the need for fulfillment of women's and children's rights, as well as the right of women's and children's participation in development;
b. collecting data and information related to the protection of women and children;
c. receiving complaints from the community;
d. conducting cooperation and coordination in implementing the fulfillment of women's and children's rights in accordance with applicable laws and regulations; ande. making referrals on cases that cannot be resolved by KPPAD/KPPAK to UPTD PPA. (4) Further provisions regarding the establishment, main duties and functions as well as management of KPPAD/KPPAK as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated in a Regent Regulation.

